{
  "id": "phrase-with-cite",
  "description": "A replication or reproduction phrase AND an author-year citation: the shape of a paper that says what it replicated. The only route to the expensive two-voter screen. `none_of` carries the GWAS vocabulary — a discovery/replication-cohort design says 'we replicated one SNP' and means something else. Divergence 3 in docs/filter-engine.md: the guard was sentence-scoped in PHRASE_GUARDS and is row-scoped here, so a GWAS-flavoured row falls to screen_cheap rather than being suppressed. The cite clause is one RE2-safe approximation of an author-year citation (parenthetical, narrative and et-al forms); it replaces extract_author_year_patterns()'s lookahead-based patterns and its bare-name blacklist. Divergence 2 in docs/filter-engine.md: the cite only orders spend, it no longer admits, so an approximation is enough. The name atom is a negated class rather than `\\w`: Python `re` reads `\\w` as Unicode-aware and RE2 (pyarrow) as ASCII, so an accented surname in the cite position — García et al. (2020) — matched one backend and not the other. The trailing `\\b` became an explicit non-digit for the same reason.",
  "match": {
    "all_of": [
      {
        "any_of": [
          {
            "text_regex": "\\breplications? of\\b"
          },
          {
            "text_regex": "\\bwe replicated\\b"
          },
          {
            "text_regex": "\\bwe replicate\\b"
          },
          {
            "text_regex": "\\bdirect replications?\\b"
          },
          {
            "text_regex": "\\bconceptual replications?\\b"
          },
          {
            "text_regex": "\\bregistered replications?\\b"
          },
          {
